{#
    A document's status, for the title row of a detail screen and for the Status cell of a list
    (queue item 41, ruled 2026-09-11: "dont hide status. status shoudl be in tables and on the
    detail page, big and loud").

    One partial rather than a span written out thirty times, because the whole point of the ruling
    is that a status looks the SAME wherever it is read. The classes are the grid's own — the
    `.order-status` pill plus the `.order-status-<slug>` modifier that `/admin/order`,
    `/admin/invoice` and the vendor bill grid already put on their Status cells — so the colour is
    decided in exactly one place (`app.css`, `.site-admin .order-status-*`) for both the list and
    the page. `.document-status` adds the size and the ring, and nothing else.

    `status` is the human string, not the enum. Callers on the status seam — SalesOrder and Estimate
    — hand in `x.status` directly; the documents still holding an enum hand in `x.status.value`. The
    day the last of them joins the seam, every caller passes the plain string. The slug is lowercased with spaces hyphened
    so "Partially Invoiced" reaches `.order-status-partially-invoiced`, which is how every grid in
    the app builds the same class.

    ## Two sizes, one badge — the list half of the same ruling

    The ruling has two halves and they want different sizes. A table cell rendering the 34px ringed
    badge is a row half again as tall as its neighbours, on a grid whose job is to be scanned. So
    the size is the ONE thing a caller chooses, and it is the only thing:

      - **loud** (the default, and what every detail screen gets by passing nothing) — the ringed
        `.document-status` pill app.css sizes for a title row;
      - **`loud: false`** — the grid's own `.order-status` pill, which is exactly the markup each
        Status cell carried before it was gathered up into here.

    Both carry `.order-status-<slug>`, so a Void bill is the same red in its row as on its page and
    there is no second table of colours anywhere. `is defined` rather than `default()` because
    `strict_variables` is on and `only` means an unpassed name does not exist at all — the same
    reason `@Procurement/_nav.html.twig` tests its optional names that way.

    ## The two anchors are deliberately different words

    `data-document-status` marks the LOUD one only. It reads as "the status of the document this
    page IS about", and a detail screen that also lists documents — the RFQ's replies, a vendor's
    documents, a customer's orders — would otherwise carry a dozen of them, so every assertion
    against that selector would quietly become ambiguous the day a list landed on a detail page.

    `data-status-cell` marks the quiet one, and it is how a test says "this cell came through the
    shared partial" rather than merely "this cell looks like it did". Hand-rolled markup can copy a
    class; this attribute is written in exactly ONE file in the repository, which is a rule a test
    can assert and a template cannot accidentally satisfy.

    Either way #627 forbids asserting the bare word: "Draft" is also in the filter dropdown of the
    very grid under test, so an assertion names the element it is about.
#}
{% set statusIsLoud = loud is not defined or loud %}
<span class="order-status{{ statusIsLoud ? ' document-status' : '' }} order-status-{{ status|lower|replace({' ': '-'}) }}"{% if statusIsLoud %} data-document-status{% else %} data-status-cell{% endif %}>{{ status }}</span>
